<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hindu Kush</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> Kush is a mountain range in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> that separates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>. Its passes were the routes used by invaders entering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from the north-west. Rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a>, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duwa">Duwa</a>, raided across it into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>'s territory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hindu-kush.jpg" alt="Hindu Kush" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ninara from Helsinki, Finland · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hindu_Kush_mountains_(30357238428).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chagataid armies also crossed the <b>Hindu Kush</b> to invade the Delhi Sultanate repeatedly from the 1290s, under commanders sent by Duwa such as his son Qutlugh Khwaja, who led a great army to the outskirts of Delhi in 1299; but Alauddin Khalji's formidable forces turned back these invasions…</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Ahmad then turned west to Mashhad, whose ruler Shahrukh Afshar acknowledged Afghan suzerainty, and sent an army north of the <b>Hindu Kush</b> that brought the Tajik, Hazara, Uzbek, Turkmen and other peoples of northern Afghanistan under his control.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Their stronghold was Tokharistan, on the northern slopes of the <b>Hindu Kush</b> in today's southern Uzbekistan and northern Afghanistan, with a capital probably at Kunduz.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The abundance of the coinage suggests large mining operations, particularly in the <b>Hindu Kush</b>, and an important monetary economy; the kings struck bilingual coins in both the Greek round standard and the Indian square standard, so money seems to have circulated through every part of society, and the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kushans were one of five branches of the Yuezhi, a people driven from the Gansu corridor by the Xiongnu in the 2nd century BCE, who settled in Bactria, north of the <b>Hindu Kush</b>, by about 130 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Defeated again, they marched south-west through Fergana and, probably in the 130s BCE, crossed into Bactria, overwhelming what remained of Greek rule north of the <b>Hindu Kush</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuezhi">Yuezhi · Military</a></li></ul></section>
